WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 85 for June 27th, 2026.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.540
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.340
Let's go. You know that feeling when you discover an old document tucked away somewhere,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.860
only to find the writing has faded beyond recognition over time.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.500
Researchers just used AI to recover a lost passage from an ancient scroll that had been buried

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.880
and damaged for 2,000 years. The text had crumbled so badly that no human eye could make out the

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:38.160
letters anymore. The AI looked at tiny remaining patterns in the fragments and predicted what words

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.440
and sentences used to be there. One recovered line mentions people who strained themselves

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.500
to the utmost through research and learning to gain practical wisdom. Think of the process

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.940
like assembling a giant jigsaw puzzle where most pieces are missing and the ones left are

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.540
badly faded. The AI does not guess at random, it has studied millions of other ancient writings first

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.700
so it understands how words and ideas typically connect. That knowledge lets it fill in the blanks

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.960
with real accuracy instead of wild invention. History stays locked away without tools like this

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.680
but the same method could one day help libraries around the world unlock faded records that

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.140
currently sit untouched. For anyone curious about the past, whether through school projects,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:30.060
family stories or archaeology, this approach turns impossible documents into readable ones.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.380
On a personal level, it acts like a super-powered detective that spots clues invisible to regular eyes.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.840
If you have ever wished you could decipher a faded family letter or an old printed page,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.780
the idea scales up from there. You cannot try the exact scroll tool yet,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.040
but the same image and text approach works on everyday notes.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.720
Open the free version of Google's Gemini app on your phone, upload a photo of any messy handwritten

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.860
note and ask it to transcribe what it sees. Pay attention to how it handles incomplete or smudge

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:09.620
sections. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.540
today's concept comes from how AI creates brand new burger recipes from scratch instead of

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:20.780
copying old ones. You know how when you are drawing with a friend and you both start with random

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.440
dots on paper? First, you add a few broad strokes that hint at a burger bun shape.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.600
Then you refine the edges so the circle looks round instead of blurry.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:35.900
Next, you layer in details like sesame seeds or a patty until the whole picture sharpens.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.120
At each step, the drawing gets clearer because you remove noise and keep what looks right.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.840
Now picture the same process, but with words instead of lines on a page.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.760
The AI begins with pure noise, like random letters scattered everywhere.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.960
It decides one tiny step at a time which word fits best in each position.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.880
It repeats the cleanup until the paragraph reads like a real recipe that no one wrote before.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:08.140
That repeated cleanup is how a diffusion model invents fresh burgers without ever copying an

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:14.840
existing menu. The same step by step trick works for pictures, music or even code once you see it

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.640
as starting messy and getting cleaner. The result stops feeling like magic and starts feeling like

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.680
a really patient artist working beside you. A model trained only on burger recipes figured out the

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.580
big Mac on its own and then created fresh combinations aimed at being extra tasty, more sustainable

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:39.020
or healthier. It didn't copy existing menus. Instead, it explored what makes a burger good

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:45.820
and invented new ones. This shows how AI can help with creative hobbies like cooking, game design or

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:51.020
even writing stories. Anyone who likes food or experimenting in the kitchen should try playing

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:57.140
with similar creative tools. Head to the free chat GPT website, start a new chat and type this

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:03.360
exact line. Act as a burger chef and invent three brand new burgers optimized for maximum

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.820
deliciousness using only common ingredients. Ask it to explain why each one works, then tweak

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:16.020
the prompt to focus on vegetarian options or low cost ingredients next. You will see how the model

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.940
builds ideas from patterns it learned rather than pulling finished recipes from a list.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:27.280
Another interesting update involves realistic AI pictures showing up in published science journals.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:33.740
Some generated images look so convincing that they can mislead readers and hurt trust in research.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.800
Journals are adding extra checks because the problem has grown large enough to notice.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.420
It serves as a reminder that not everything you see online came from a human with a camera.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:50.640
To practice spotting the difference yourself, go to the free Bing image creator at Bing.com

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:57.940
slash images slash create. Type a simple prompt like a scientist looking through a microscope in a lab

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:03.780
compare the results side by side with a real photo from a news site and notice which small details

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:10.760
look slightly off. In QuickBits this week, BiteDans released an 8 billion parameter model called

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:17.680
ILLADA. The system generates text through an approach that differs from the methods used in chat

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.980
GPT style systems. Despite the new method, the model reaches performance levels that align with

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:30.040
certain leading models when evaluated on basic tests. Its results demonstrate that multiple routes

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:36.520
exist for constructing capable AI tools. The company selected a diffusion-based design for

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.540
handling language tasks. Overall, the launch underscores continued variety and techniques for

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:49.520
creating useful AI. Open AI's newest model went through hundreds of thousands of GPU hours of

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.260
testing plus human red teaming before release. The evaluation process targeted prevention of

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:02.200
cyber misuse and repeated harmful behavior. Extensive checks occurred as models increase in

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:08.920
capability. The effort combined automated runs with direct human review sessions. Focus remained on

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.840
reducing risks tied to more advanced systems. Results from the testing informed final adjustments

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.440
prior to public availability. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go,

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:26.800
huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow.

